WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.441
Flipkart-Style Sale-Day E-Commerce.

2
00:00:02.721 --> 00:00:07.627
Chapter I02, from the Industry Designs part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:10.337
At a glance:

4
00:00:10.617 --> 00:00:11.791
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:12.071 --> 00:00:12.881
Sector:

6
00:00:13.161 --> 00:00:15.977
E-commerce (India, festive sales), Reader bands:

7
00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:18.305
B1, then B4, Builds on:

8
00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:24.153
Ch 02, Ch 45, Ch 46, Ch 49, I01.

9
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:32.945
What you get: the design of an Indian e-commerce platform for its biggest days: a multi-day festive sale with midnight deal unlocks.

10
00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:45.279
It covers what is specific to India: cash on delivery and parcels returned undelivered;. pincode serviceability;. bank offers and no-cost EMI;. slow networks and low-end phones;.

11
00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:54.863
vernacular shoppers;. reseller rings on limited deals;. the operating model of a sale: readiness, war rooms and change freezes.

12
00:00:55.143 --> 00:01:02.439
The waiting room and hot-SKU reservation are in Ch 46, and payment routing is in Ch 45.

13
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:05.621
This chapter builds on them rather than repeating them.

14
00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:07.159
What the labs measured:

15
00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:21.885
L1 (deal unlock): with a million phones whose clocks are wrong, polling the origin every 2 s costs 102M requests and gets only 37% of shoppers within 1 s of the unlock.

16
00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:24.000
Trusting the phone clock is worse:

17
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.485
17.5% tap early and 12% are late by more than 5 s.

18
00:01:29.765 --> 00:01:38.512
Syncing from the server's clock gets 96.6%, and a millisecond re-sync at Tminus 10 s gets 100%.

19
00:01:38.792 --> 00:01:48.968
L2 (COD policy, illustrative economics): turning off COD loses a third of orders, and is the worst policy once new-customer value counts.

20
00:01:49.248 --> 00:01:58.869
OTP confirmation for medium-risk orders cuts parcels returned undelivered (RTO) by half and gives the best result on both measures.

21
00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:09.883
L3 (page weight, real compression): a 40-product listing is 65 KB of full JSON and 1.5 KB trimmed and gzipped.

22
00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:19.955
On 2G that is 8.0 s versus 2.9 s on a new connection, and 5.9 s versus 0.8 s on a reused one.

23
00:02:20.235 --> 00:02:23.201
Connection setup dominates once payloads are small.

24
00:02:23.481 --> 00:02:32.953
L4 (reseller rings): per-account and exact-address limits let a 400-account ring take 800 and 778 deal units.

25
00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:48.998
A normalised tower or flat or pincode key cuts that to 24, the theoretical minimum of 2 per drop point, while blocking 82 genuine buyers against an unavoidable floor of 82 (same household or the drop point itself).

26
00:02:49.278 --> 00:02:52.137
Fuzzy text similarity is worse on both counts.

27
00:02:53.487 --> 00:02:54.468
Reading time:

28
00:02:54.748 --> 00:02:55.644
80 min.

29
00:02:56.994 --> 00:02:57.741
Warning:

30
00:02:58.021 --> 00:02:59.898
What this chapter is, and isn't.

31
00:03:00.178 --> 00:03:09.949
This is a design in the style of a large Indian e-commerce platform during festive sales, based on public talks, engineering blogs and news coverage.

32
00:03:10.229 --> 00:03:13.535
It is not a description of Flipkart's internal systems.

33
00:03:13.815 --> 00:03:19.469
Business numbers (COD share, RTO rates, costs, network profiles) are illustrative.

34
00:03:19.749 --> 00:03:22.373
Regulatory statements are marked VERIFY.

35
00:03:23.723 --> 00:03:24.576
Part 1.

36
00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:26.776
Introduction and Why It Matters.

37
00:03:28.026 --> 00:03:31.973
A story: midnight, and the phone that thought it was 11:58.

38
00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:42.471
The sale's headline deal, a phone at 9,999 rupeeswith 5,000 units, unlocks at 12:00:00.

39
00:03:42.751 --> 00:03:43.861
The early taps.

40
00:03:44.141 --> 00:03:51.671
At 11:59:58 the origin receives 40,000 Buy requests from phones whose clocks run fast.

41
00:03:51.951 --> 00:04:00.207
They are rejected, the apps retry, and the retries land at 12:00:00, exactly when the real demand arrives.

42
00:04:00.487 --> 00:04:01.639
The late shoppers.

43
00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:03.690
Some shoppers' phones run slow.

44
00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:08.919
Their Buy button appears at 12:00:40, after the units have gone.

45
00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:10.181
The complaints.

46
00:04:10.461 --> 00:04:12.999
Social media fills with the sale was rigged.

47
00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:15.181
The ring.

48
00:04:15.461 --> 00:04:22.971
A reseller ring with 400 accounts ships 800 units to a dozen flats, with each address typed a different way.

49
00:04:23.251 --> 00:04:24.232
The returns.

50
00:04:24.512 --> 00:04:32.875
Three days later, 18% of the cash-on-delivery orders in some regions come back undelivered, and the reverse shipping eats the margin.

51
00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:35.352
None of this is a database problem.

52
00:04:35.632 --> 00:04:40.752
It is time, trust, networks and money, and in India each of these has its own shape.

53
00:04:42.102 --> 00:04:42.785
Quote:

54
00:04:43.065 --> 00:04:44.729
The thesis of this chapter.

55
00:04:45.009 --> 00:04:48.721
An Indian sale day is won before it starts and at the edges.

56
00:04:49.001 --> 00:04:52.755
Synchronise clients to server time instead of polling (L1).

57
00:04:53.035 --> 00:04:56.662
Price the risk of cash on delivery instead of banning it (L2).

58
00:04:56.942 --> 00:05:00.078
Design for 2G and cold connections (L3).

59
00:05:00.358 --> 00:05:03.835
Enforce deal limits on the real delivery address (L4).

60
00:05:04.115 --> 00:05:10.281
Behind all of that: the capacity, waiting room and reservation machinery of Ch 46.

61
00:05:11.631 --> 00:05:13.103
What's specific to India.

