Vernacular UI and search, transliteration.

79
00:06:02.431 --> 00:06:04.265
Where: §2.7.

80
00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:06.700
UPI, cards, EMI, bank offers.

81
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.175
Effect on design:

82
00:06:08.455 --> 00:06:10.993
Payment mix, offer eligibility at checkout.

83
00:06:11.273 --> 00:06:14.153
Where: §2.8, Ch 45.

84
00:06:15.503 --> 00:06:17.274
Festive season concentration.

85
00:06:17.554 --> 00:06:18.749
Effect on design:

86
00:06:19.029 --> 00:06:21.610
A large share of annual sales in a few days.

87
00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.661
Where: §2.9.

88
00:06:23.941 --> 00:06:26.287
Consumer-protection and e-commerce rules.

89
00:06:26.567 --> 00:06:27.762
Effect on design:

90
00:06:28.042 --> 00:06:33.546
Display of seller details, country of origin, flash-sale rules (VERIFY current rules).

91
00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.725
Where: §6.5.

92
00:06:37.075 --> 00:06:38.397
Learning objectives.

93
00:06:38.677 --> 00:06:40.576
By the end you will be able to:

94
00:06:40.856 --> 00:06:43.885
Design a sale-day architecture and readiness plan.

95
00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:47.451
Synchronise millions of clients to a deal unlock fairly.

96
00:06:47.731 --> 00:06:52.296
Decide COD eligibility with risk and economics, not blanket rules.

97
00:06:52.576 --> 00:06:55.520
Budget payloads and connections for slow networks.

98
00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.195
Enforce purchase limits against reseller rings without blocking households.

99
00:07:01.545 --> 00:07:02.419
Part 2.

100
00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:04.577
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

101
00:07:05.827 --> 00:07:09.197
Intuition: a festival market that opens at midnight.

102
00:07:09.477 --> 00:07:15.301
Imagine a market where every shop opens at the stroke of midnight and the best items go in seconds.

103
00:07:15.581 --> 00:07:17.459
Some shoppers' watches are wrong.

104
00:07:17.739 --> 00:07:19.317
Some came on slow buses.

105
00:07:19.597 --> 00:07:22.648
Some pay in cash at their door and change their minds.

106
00:07:22.928 --> 00:07:27.301
And a few professional buyers send twenty cousins to queue for the same item.

107
00:07:27.581 --> 00:07:39.123
A good market organiser fixes all of these before midnight: one clock everyone trusts, a door that admits people in order, clear rules on cash, and limits per household, not per person.

108
00:07:40.473 --> 00:07:42.073
Requirements and estimates.

109
00:07:42.353 --> 00:07:43.697
Here is a quick table:

110
00:07:43.977 --> 00:07:45.278
Type, and Requirement.

111
00:07:45.558 --> 00:07:46.433
Functional:

112
00:07:46.713 --> 00:08:00.345
Deal pages with countdowns; sale-only prices and bank offers; limited-quantity deals with per-customer limits; cart; checkout with UPI, cards, EMI, wallets and COD; order tracking; returns.

113
00:08:00.625 --> 00:08:01.883
Scale (assumptions):

114
00:08:02.163 --> 00:08:14.110
100M app opens on day 1; at peak minutes, ~1M concurrent shoppers on the deal page; ~10 to 20 times normal checkout rate (VERIFY with your own history).

115
00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:15.307
Latency:

116
00:08:15.587 --> 00:08:27.235
Deal page usable within 3 s on a slow 3G connection; Buy button enabled within ±1 s of unlock for ≥ 99% of shoppers.

117
00:08:27.515 --> 00:08:28.390
Fairness:

118
00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:35.262
First to tap is first served (within network variance); per-customer limits enforced on the delivery household.

119
00:08:35.542 --> 00:08:36.459
Correctness:

120
00:08:36.739 --> 00:08:42.201
No overselling deal units (Ch 46); the bank offer shown is the one applied.

121
00:08:43.551 --> 00:08:45.620
Estimation (Ch 02):

122
00:08:45.900 --> 00:09:00.044
1M shoppers tapping Buy within ~2 seconds of unlock gives ~500k requests per second for a few seconds at the origin (L1: ~580 to 680k in the peak second).

123
00:09:00.324 --> 00:09:10.927
That is why the tap must be admitted through a waiting room and reserved against sharded stock (Ch 46), and why nothing else may hit the origin at that second (L1).

124
00:09:12.277 --> 00:09:13.685
Architecture overview.

125
00:09:13.965 --> 00:09:15.309
Here is a quick table:

126
00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:17.679
Component, and Role on sale day.

127
00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:19.261
Edge or CDN:

128
00:09:19.541 --> 00:09:23.978
Static assets, deal pages, and the edge-cached time endpoint (L1).

129
00:09:24.258 --> 00:09:25.303
Deal service:

130
00:09:25.583 --> 00:09:29.871
Deal definitions, unlock times, quantity pools, per-customer limits.

131
00:09:30.151 --> 00:09:31.197
Time service:

132
00:09:31.477 --> 00:09:33.930
Millisecond server time served from the edge.

133
00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:35.789
Waiting room and admission:

134
00:09:36.069 --> 00:09:40.037
Fair entry to checkout at a sustainable rate (Ch 46).

135
00:09:40.317 --> 00:09:41.831
Inventory reservation:

136
00:09:42.111 --> 00:09:46.037
Bucketed deal stock, holds with a TTL (Ch 46).

137
00:09:46.317 --> 00:09:47.853
Pricing and offers engine:

138
00:09:48.133 --> 00:09:53.338
Sale prices, bank instant discounts, EMI plans, coupons (Ch 49).

139
00:09:53.618 --> 00:09:54.962
Checkout and payments:

140
00:09:55.242 --> 00:09:59.679
UPI, cards, EMI, wallets, COD; gateway routing (Ch 45).

141
00:09:59.959 --> 00:10:01.069
Risk and abuse:

142
00:10:01.349 --> 00:10:05.701
Reseller detection (L4), COD risk (L2), payment fraud.

143
00:10:07.051 --> 00:10:08.629
Serviceability and promise:

144
00:10:08.909 --> 00:10:13.667
Pincode coverage, delivery dates (Ch 01, I01 L3).

145
00:10:13.947 --> 00:10:15.333
Order and logistics:

146
00:10:15.613 --> 00:10:19.709
Manifests, courier allocation, returns, cash reconciliation.

147
00:10:19.989 --> 00:10:21.099
Notifications:

148
00:10:21.379 --> 00:10:25.496
Sale reminders, order updates, with priority lanes (Ch 40).

149
00:10:26.846 --> 00:10:28.979
Unlocking a deal on a million phones.

150
00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:33.377
Polling the origin to ask is it live? is the worst option (L1):

151
00:10:33.657 --> 00:10:42.147
102M requests in five minutes, and still only 37% of shoppers ready within a second, because of the poll interval.

152
00:10:42.427 --> 00:10:46.950
The phone's own clock is wrong for a meaningful minority (L1's assumption:

153
00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.641
30% are off by more than a second).

154
00:10:49.921 --> 00:10:58.241
So 17.5% tap early (rejected, then retried at the worst moment), and 12% are more than 5 s late.

155
00:10:58.521 --> 00:11:00.377
That is the rigged sale complaint.

156
00:11:00.657 --> 00:11:02.129
Sync from the server once.

157
00:11:03.479 --> 00:11:13.377
On page load, compute offset = serverTime minus localTime, using the response's Date header or a time field, and correct for half the round trip.

158
00:11:13.657 --> 00:11:20.569
The Date header has one-second resolution, which gives 96.6% within ±1 s.

159
00:11:20.849 --> 00:11:28.743
Re-sync at Tminus 10 s from an edge-cached, millisecond-precision time endpoint (measure the round trip, use its midpoint).

160
00:11:29.023 --> 00:11:32.329
That gives 100% within ±1 s.

161
00:11:32.609 --> 00:11:33.441
Also:

162
00:11:34.791 --> 00:11:37.821
Pre-fetch the deal page and assets before unlock.

163
00:11:38.101 --> 00:11:39.615
Enable the button locally.

164
00:11:39.895 --> 00:11:46.637
The server still checks the time authoritatively on the Buy request, and rejects early taps without penalising them.

165
00:11:46.917 --> 00:11:54.042
The unavoidable part is the Buy spike itself (~0.6M requests per second in the peak second).

166
00:11:54.322 --> 00:11:57.053
It goes to the waiting room, not straight to checkout.

167
00:11:58.403 --> 00:12:00.280
Cash on delivery and RTO.

168
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.077
COD lets the customer pay at the door.

169
00:12:03.357 --> 00:12:08.115
It widens reach (new customers, customers without cards, low trust), but adds:

170
00:12:08.395 --> 00:12:15.797
RTO: the parcel is refused or undeliverable and travels back, so you pay both shipping legs and make no sale.

171
00:12:16.077 --> 00:12:21.005
Cash handling and reconciliation (Ch 45 §2.7).

172
00:12:21.285 --> 00:12:24.379
The risk model scores each order's chance of RTO.

173
00:12:24.659 --> 00:12:32.552
Illustrative features: pincode tier, whether the customer is new, their past RTO history, order value, and time to delivery.

174
00:12:32.832 --> 00:12:34.091
Actions by score:

175
00:12:34.371 --> 00:12:35.309
Low risk:

176
00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:37.147
COD offered normally.

177
00:12:38.497 --> 00:12:39.542
Medium risk:

178
00:12:39.822 --> 00:12:46.307
COD with confirmation, by OTP or IVR before dispatch, or a small partial prepayment.

179
00:12:46.587 --> 00:12:48.337
High risk: prepaid only.

180
00:12:48.617 --> 00:12:51.305
L2 findings (illustrative economics):

181
00:12:51.585 --> 00:12:59.478
Blocking COD entirely keeps only 68% of orders and is worst once the future value of new customers counts.

182
00:12:59.758 --> 00:13:05.539
Aggressive blocking (P3, P4) raises this order's profit but gives up future value.

183
00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:13.481
The best policy blocks only very high risk and confirms the middle band (P5): best on both measures.

184
00:13:13.761 --> 00:13:22.679
Operations matter too: a promise that slips raises refusals, and delivery attempt scheduling and customer calls reduce RTO.

185
00:13:22.959 --> 00:13:25.924
Measure RTO by pincode, courier and seller.

186
00:13:27.274 --> 00:13:29.471
Slow networks and entry-level phones.

187
00:13:29.751 --> 00:13:31.394
Budget the payload per screen.

188
00:13:31.674 --> 00:13:32.975
L3's list view:

189
00:13:33.255 --> 00:13:40.317
65 KB of full JSON shrinks to 1.5 KB when trimmed to the rendered fields and gzipped.

190
00:13:40.597 --> 00:13:43.519
On 2G the connection costs more than the bytes.

191
00:13:43.799 --> 00:13:54.210
A new HTTPS connection costs 3 round trips of DNS, TCP and TLS, which is 2.1 s at 700 ms RTT.

192
00:13:54.490 --> 00:14:04.175
Reusing connections, limiting the number of origins, and HTTP or 2 or HTTP or 3 multiplexing matter as much as compression.

193
00:14:04.455 --> 00:14:10.493
Trimmed + gzip took 2.9 s on a new connection and 0.8 s on a reused one.

194
00:14:11.843 --> 00:14:20.419
Images dominate real pages: list thumbnails in modern formats at the rendered size, lazy loading, and low-quality placeholders.

195
00:14:20.699 --> 00:14:24.688
Lite apps and PWAs for low-end phones and slow networks.

196
00:14:24.968 --> 00:14:31.496
Flipkart publicly launched a PWA, Flipkart Lite, in 2015 (VERIFY details).

197
00:14:31.776 --> 00:14:37.813
Offline-tolerant cart and retries with idempotency keys, because connections drop mid-checkout.

198
00:14:39.163 --> 00:14:40.507
Vernacular shoppers.

199
00:14:40.787 --> 00:14:47.251
UI localisation for many Indian languages, with content from the catalog in multiple languages.

200
00:14:47.531 --> 00:15:03.190
Search: handle transliteration (mobail / मोबाइल, then mobile), mixed-script queries and voice input;. query understanding maps them to the same catalog (Ch 22, Ch 42);. measure search success per language.

201
00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:09.191
Keep font and layout budgets in mind for complex scripts on low-end phones.

202
00:15:10.541 --> 00:15:13.271
Bank offers, EMI and the price at checkout.

203
00:15:13.551 --> 00:15:22.810
Sale prices often come with bank instant discounts (for example, 10% off with specific cards, up to a cap) and no-cost EMI.

204
00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:29.319
In no-cost EMI, the interest is funded as a discount (VERIFY accounting and disclosure rules).

205
00:15:29.599 --> 00:15:37.685
Eligibility is only known at payment time, from the card BIN or UPI app, bank, EMI tenure and cap usage.

206
00:15:37.965 --> 00:15:51.959
So the checkout must: show the estimated offer early;. recompute exactly when the instrument is chosen;. reserve the bank's offer budget atomically (per-bank daily caps are the budget-pacing problem of Ch 49);.

207
00:15:53.309 --> 00:15:55.059
release it on payment failure.

208
00:15:55.339 --> 00:16:04.235
Price integrity: the order stores the price, each discount line and who funds it (seller, platform, bank) for settlement and invoicing (GST).

209
00:16:05.585 --> 00:16:07.761
Reseller rings and purchase limits.

210
00:16:08.041 --> 00:16:15.123
Limited deals (2 per customer) attract rings: many accounts, cards and phones, but few real delivery points.

211
00:16:15.403 --> 00:16:16.278
L4:

212
00:16:16.558 --> 00:16:19.843
Per-account limits give the ring everything, 800 units.

213
00:16:20.123 --> 00:16:26.011
Exact-address matching gives it 778, because the ring types each address differently.

214
00:16:26.291 --> 00:16:34.035
Normalising to tower + flat + pincode gives it exactly 24, which is 2 per drop point, the minimum possible.

215
00:16:35.385 --> 00:16:39.716
It blocks 82 genuine buyers, exactly the unavoidable cases:

216
00:16:39.996 --> 00:16:45.500
79 second buyers in the same household, and 3 who live in the drop-point flats.

217
00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.700
Fuzzy text similarity is worse:

218
00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:59.735
Flat 1102 and Flat 1103 look 80% similar, so it merged neighbours (2,034 blocked) and still let 202 units through.

219
00:17:00.015 --> 00:17:03.833
Parse structure first; use fuzzy matching only on leftovers.

220
00:17:04.113 --> 00:17:10.023
Combine signals: device, payment instrument, phone number, address, and graph links between accounts.

221
00:17:10.303 --> 00:17:13.929
Enforce at checkout (reservation) and again before dispatch.

222
00:17:14.209 --> 00:17:18.369
Communicate household limits clearly so genuine families understand.

223
00:17:19.719 --> 00:17:22.023
Readiness: running a sale like a launch.

224
