00:37:48.417 --> 00:37:49.207
Effect:

437
00:37:49.487 --> 00:37:51.599
Early and late taps, rigged complaints.

438
00:37:51.879 --> 00:37:52.881
Mitigation:

439
00:37:53.161 --> 00:37:55.743
Server-time sync + re-sync (L1).

440
00:37:56.023 --> 00:37:57.047
Buy spike.

441
00:37:57.327 --> 00:37:58.116
Effect:

442
00:37:58.396 --> 00:37:59.633
Checkout overload.

443
00:37:59.913 --> 00:38:00.916
Mitigation:

444
00:38:01.196 --> 00:38:04.588
Waiting room, bucketed reservation (Ch 46).

445
00:38:04.868 --> 00:38:06.681
Gateway or bank degraded.

446
00:38:06.961 --> 00:38:07.751
Effect:

447
00:38:08.031 --> 00:38:09.140
Payment failures.

448
00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:10.423
Mitigation:

449
00:38:10.703 --> 00:38:13.988
Routing and health-based switching (Ch 45).

450
00:38:14.268 --> 00:38:16.871
OTP or SMS provider throttled.

451
00:38:17.151 --> 00:38:17.940
Effect:

452
00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:20.652
Logins and COD confirmations fail.

453
00:38:20.932 --> 00:38:21.935
Mitigation:

454
00:38:22.215 --> 00:38:25.863
Multiple providers, WhatsApp or IVR fallback, queueing.

455
00:38:26.143 --> 00:38:27.209
Reseller ring.

456
00:38:27.489 --> 00:38:28.279
Effect:

457
00:38:28.559 --> 00:38:30.351
Genuine customers miss deals.

458
00:38:30.631 --> 00:38:31.633
Mitigation:

459
00:38:31.913 --> 00:38:35.284
Household keys (L4), device and payment graph.

460
00:38:35.564 --> 00:38:36.759
RTO surge.

461
00:38:37.039 --> 00:38:37.828
Effect:

462
00:38:38.108 --> 00:38:39.132
Margin loss.

463
00:38:39.412 --> 00:38:40.415
Mitigation:

464
00:38:40.695 --> 00:38:43.212
Risk bands (L2), promise accuracy.

465
00:38:44.562 --> 00:38:45.650
Observability.

466
00:38:45.930 --> 00:39:02.101
The war-room dashboards show: journey success rates: browse, add to cart, checkout, payment by method and bank;. waiting-room depth and admission rate;. deal units remaining;. time-sync error from clients (reported by the app);. payload sizes by screen;.

467
00:39:02.381 --> 00:39:05.751
RTO and COD confirmation rates by region;.

468
00:39:07.101 --> 00:39:08.829
limit hits and abuse blocks.

469
00:39:10.179 --> 00:39:11.545
Security and abuse.

470
00:39:11.825 --> 00:39:19.249
Bot traffic on deal pages is handled by device attestation, behavioural checks and rate limits (Ch 39).

471
00:39:19.529 --> 00:39:23.497
Account takeover spikes around sales need step-up authentication.

472
00:39:23.777 --> 00:39:27.702
Promo and bank-offer abuse is handled by per-instrument caps.

473
00:39:29.052 --> 00:39:30.396
Performance and cost.

474
00:39:30.676 --> 00:39:35.369
Pre-scaling and reserved capacity for the sale window (Ch 35).

475
00:39:35.649 --> 00:39:40.193
Payload budgets (L3) reduce CDN egress as well as latency.

476
00:39:40.473 --> 00:39:44.505
RTO is a logistics cost line to track per region (L2).

477
00:39:45.855 --> 00:39:46.751
Compliance.

478
00:39:47.031 --> 00:40:00.962
E-commerce rules on seller information display, country of origin, grievance officers, and restrictions on certain flash-sale practices (VERIFY the current Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules and amendments).

479
00:40:01.242 --> 00:40:03.269
Invoicing with GST per seller.

480
00:40:03.549 --> 00:40:09.202
Payment data rules (RBI tokenisation for cards; VERIFY) (Ch 36).

481
00:40:10.552 --> 00:40:11.491
Part 7.

482
00:40:11.771 --> 00:40:13.477
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

483
00:40:14.727 --> 00:40:15.645
Watch out:

484
00:40:15.925 --> 00:40:16.565
1.

485
00:40:16.845 --> 00:40:18.935
Polling the origin for deal status.

486
00:40:19.215 --> 00:40:22.074
It costs 100M requests for nothing (L1).

487
00:40:23.424 --> 00:40:24.341
Watch out:

488
00:40:24.621 --> 00:40:25.325
2.

489
00:40:25.605 --> 00:40:26.992
Trusting the phone's clock.

490
00:40:27.272 --> 00:40:29.128
Early and late taps (L1).

491
00:40:30.478 --> 00:40:31.395
Watch out:

492
00:40:31.675 --> 00:40:32.422
3.

493
00:40:32.702 --> 00:40:34.750
Rejecting early taps with a penalty.

494
00:40:35.030 --> 00:40:37.163
It punishes clock skew, not cheating.

495
00:40:38.513 --> 00:40:39.431
Watch out:

496
00:40:39.711 --> 00:40:40.415
4.

497
00:40:40.695 --> 00:40:42.956
Turning off COD to cut RTO.

498
00:40:43.236 --> 00:40:45.455
It loses a third of orders (L2).

499
00:40:46.805 --> 00:40:47.722
Watch out:

500
00:40:48.002 --> 00:40:48.770
5.

501
00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:52.335
Judging COD policy on this order's profit only.

502
00:40:52.615 --> 00:40:55.111
It ignores new-customer value (L2).

503
00:40:56.461 --> 00:40:57.379
Watch out:

504
00:40:57.659 --> 00:40:58.405
6.

505
00:40:58.685 --> 00:41:01.224
Shipping full product JSON to list views.

506
00:41:01.504 --> 00:41:03.083
Payload bloat (L3).

507
00:41:04.433 --> 00:41:05.350
Watch out:

508
00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:06.377
7.

509
00:41:06.657 --> 00:41:08.833
Many origins per screen on 2G.

510
00:41:09.113 --> 00:41:11.395
Connection setup dominates (L3).

511
00:41:12.745 --> 00:41:13.663
Watch out:

512
00:41:13.943 --> 00:41:14.625
8.

513
00:41:14.905 --> 00:41:16.313
Per-account deal limits.

514
00:41:16.593 --> 00:41:18.023
Rings win (L4).

515
00:41:19.373 --> 00:41:20.290
Watch out:

516
00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:21.317
9.

517
00:41:21.597 --> 00:41:23.815
Fuzzy address matching as the main key.

518
00:41:24.095 --> 00:41:25.866
It blocks neighbours (L4).

519
00:41:27.216 --> 00:41:28.133
Watch out:

520
00:41:28.413 --> 00:41:29.117
10.

521
00:41:29.397 --> 00:41:32.448
Estimating bank offers without reserving the budget.

522
00:41:32.728 --> 00:41:33.944
Caps are overspent.

523
00:41:35.294 --> 00:41:36.211
Watch out:

524
00:41:36.491 --> 00:41:37.323
11.

525
00:41:37.603 --> 00:41:39.075
Changes during the sale.

526
00:41:39.355 --> 00:41:40.998
Freeze critical services.

527
00:41:42.348 --> 00:41:43.265
Watch out:

528
00:41:43.545 --> 00:41:44.335
12.

529
00:41:44.615 --> 00:41:48.647
Unagreed peak volumes with banks, SMS providers and couriers.

530
00:41:48.927 --> 00:41:50.591
Dependencies fail first.

531
00:41:51.941 --> 00:41:52.815
Part 8.

532
00:41:53.095 --> 00:41:54.439
Interview Q and A.

533
00:41:55.689 --> 00:41:56.521
Beginner.

534
00:41:56.801 --> 00:41:57.825
Question 1.

535
00:41:58.105 --> 00:42:01.391
What makes a big Indian sale day different from a normal day?

536
00:42:01.671 --> 00:42:03.036
Thirty-second answer:

537
00:42:03.316 --> 00:42:19.657
Traffic spikes of an order of magnitude or more concentrated at deal unlocks, limited-quantity deals, heavy COD and slow networks outside metros, bank offers and EMI at checkout, and dependencies (banks, SMS, couriers) that also peak.

538
00:42:21.007 --> 00:42:22.010
Question 2.

539
00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:26.151
How would you show a countdown that ends at the same moment for everyone?

540
00:42:26.431 --> 00:42:27.797
Thirty-second answer:

541
00:42:28.077 --> 00:42:39.469
Send the unlock time and server time with the page, compute the phone's clock offset, re-sync precisely shortly before unlock, and let the server check the time again on the Buy request.

542
00:42:40.819 --> 00:42:41.864
Question 3.

543
00:42:42.144 --> 00:42:44.299
What is RTO and why does it matter?

544
00:42:44.579 --> 00:42:45.944
Thirty-second answer:

545
00:42:46.224 --> 00:42:57.488
Return to origin, the parcel isn't delivered (refused or unreachable) and comes back; you pay shipping both ways and make no sale, so it's a major cost for COD orders.

546
00:42:58.838 --> 00:42:59.905
Question 4.

547
00:43:00.185 --> 00:43:02.553
How would you keep the app usable on 2G?

548
00:43:02.833 --> 00:43:04.198
Thirty-second answer:

549
00:43:04.478 --> 00:43:15.038
Send only what the screen renders, compress, reuse connections and limit origins, right-size images, cache aggressively, and consider a lite app or PWA.

550
00:43:16.388 --> 00:43:17.391
Intermediate.

551
00:43:17.671 --> 00:43:18.780
Question 5.

552
00:43:19.060 --> 00:43:22.409
Design the deal-unlock flow for a million concurrent shoppers.

553
00:43:22.689 --> 00:43:24.055
Thirty-second answer:

554
00:43:24.335 --> 00:43:45.433
Deal pages and assets from the CDN before unlock; server-time sync on load and a millisecond re-sync from an edge time endpoint at Tminus 10 s; local enable at unlock; Buy goes to a waiting room that admits to checkout at a sustainable rate; the server rechecks time and per-customer limits; bucketed stock reservation.

555
00:43:46.783 --> 00:43:47.893
Question 6.

556
00:43:48.173 --> 00:43:51.181
How do you decide whether to offer COD on an order?

557
00:43:51.461 --> 00:43:52.826
Thirty-second answer:

558
00:43:53.106 --> 00:44:11.303
Score RTO risk from features like region, customer history and value; offer plain COD at low risk, COD with OTP or IVR confirmation in the middle, prepaid-only at high risk; evaluate policies on this order's margin and on new-customer value.

559
00:44:12.653 --> 00:44:13.741
Question 7.

560
00:44:14.021 --> 00:44:16.624
How are bank offers applied correctly at checkout?

561
00:44:16.904 --> 00:44:18.269
Thirty-second answer:

562
00:44:18.549 --> 00:44:34.699
Show an estimate early, recompute when the payment instrument is known (card BIN, bank, EMI tenure), reserve the offer budget atomically against daily caps, release on failure, and store each discount line with its funder for settlement and invoicing.

563
00:44:36.049 --> 00:44:37.073
Question 8.

564
00:44:37.353 --> 00:44:39.806
How do you support shoppers in many languages?

565
00:44:40.086 --> 00:44:41.451
Thirty-second answer:

566
00:44:41.731 --> 00:44:51.779
Localised UI and catalog content, search that handles transliteration, mixed scripts and voice mapping to the same catalog, and success metrics per language.

567
00:44:53.129 --> 00:44:54.196
Question 9.

568
00:44:54.476 --> 00:44:57.399
How would you prepare external dependencies for a sale?

569
00:44:57.679 --> 00:44:59.044
Thirty-second answer:

570
00:44:59.324 --> 00:45:15.516
Agree peak volumes with banks, gateways, SMS providers and couriers; route across providers; build fallbacks (IVR or WhatsApp OTP, delayed confirmations); watch per-dependency success on the war-room dashboard with switch thresholds.

571
00:45:16.866 --> 00:45:17.634
Senior.

572
00:45:17.914 --> 00:45:18.959
Question 10.

573
00:45:19.239 --> 00:45:20.925
A deal says '2 per customer'.

574
00:45:21.205 --> 00:45:24.554
How do you stop reseller rings without blocking families?

575
00:45:24.834 --> 00:45:26.199
Thirty-second answer:

576
00:45:26.479 --> 00:45:43.930
Enforce limits on the delivery household, not the account: normalise addresses into structured keys (tower, flat, pincode) and combine with device, payment and phone signals; use fuzzy matching only for unparsed leftovers; explain household limits to customers.

577
00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:46.411
Question 11.

578
00:45:46.691 --> 00:45:51.043
Why does trusting the phone clock produce complaints even when servers are fine?

579
00:45:51.323 --> 00:45:52.688
Thirty-second answer:

580
00:45:52.968 --> 00:46:03.229
A minority of phones are seconds to minutes off: fast ones tap early and get rejected, slow ones see the button after stock is gone; to users the sale looks rigged.

581
00:46:03.509 --> 00:46:06.069
Server-time sync removes almost all of it.

582
00:46:07.419 --> 00:46:08.507
Question 12.

583
00:46:08.787 --> 00:46:12.819
On a slow network, what matters more: payload size or connections?

584
00:46:13.099 --> 00:46:14.465
Thirty-second answer:

585
00:46:14.745 --> 00:46:33.305
Both, but once payloads are trimmed and compressed, connection setup dominates, a new HTTPS connection costs several round trips (2 plus s on 2G); reuse connections, reduce origins, use HTTP or 2 or HTTP or 3, and pre-connect before the user needs it.

586
00:46:34.655 --> 00:46:35.935
Question 13.

587
00:46:36.215 --> 00:46:39.287
How do you reduce RTO beyond blocking COD?

588
00:46:39.567 --> 00:46:40.932
Thirty-second answer:

589
00:46:41.212 --> 00:46:57.191
Confirmation for medium-risk orders, accurate promises (late deliveries get refused), better addresses (validation, geocoding), delivery attempt scheduling and customer calls, incentives to prepay, and courier or pincode performance management.

590
00:46:58.541 --> 00:46:59.735
Question 14.

591
00:47:00.015 --> 00:47:01.914
What does your sale-day war room look at?

592
00:47:02.194 --> 00:47:03.559
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:47:03.839 --> 00:47:18.517
Journey success rates, waiting-room depth and admission, deal inventory, payment success by method, bank and gateway, dependency health, error budgets, and abuse or limit hits, with a named decision owner per journey and pre-agreed switches.

594
00:47:19.867 --> 00:47:20.763
Architect.

595
00:47:21.043 --> 00:47:22.237
Question 15.

596
00:47:22.517 --> 00:47:25.333
Plan the engineering readiness for a festive sale.

597
00:47:25.613 --> 00:47:26.979
Thirty-second answer:

598
00:47:27.259 --> 00:47:43.088
Forecast journeys and dependency peaks, capacity plan with headroom, full-journey load and chaos tests, degradation switches, change freeze, pre-scaling and cache warming, war room with owners and comms, then a retrospective feeding next year's forecast.

599
00:47:44.438 --> 00:47:45.633
Question 16.

600
00:47:45.913 --> 00:47:50.073
How would you evaluate a proposal to stop offering COD nationally?

601
00:47:50.353 --> 00:47:51.718
Thirty-second answer:

602
00:47:51.998 --> 00:48:09.577
Model the whole economics: orders lost (not every COD customer prepays), RTO saved, and the lifetime value of new customers who only start with COD; compare with targeted risk bands; pilot by region with shadow scoring before any national change.

603
00:48:10.927 --> 00:48:12.271
Question 17.

604
00:48:12.551 --> 00:48:14.940
How do you make limited deals perceived as fair?

605
00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:16.585
Thirty-second answer:

606
00:48:16.865 --> 00:48:31.415
Synchronised unlocks, first-come order via a waiting room, household limits against rings, clear communication (position in queue, sold-out quickly), post-sale transparency on units, and no penalties for honest early taps.

607
00:48:32.765 --> 00:48:33.938
Question 18.

608
00:48:34.218 --> 00:48:39.402
How would you architect for entry-level phones and slow networks across the whole platform?

609
00:48:39.682 --> 00:48:41.047
Thirty-second answer:

610
00:48:41.327 --> 00:48:59.951
Per-screen payload and request budgets enforced in CI, BFFs shaping responses per client, image pipeline with device-aware variants, connection reuse and few origins, offline-tolerant flows with idempotency, and a lite client; measure real-user performance by network type.

611
00:49:01.301 --> 00:49:02.539
Question 19.

612
00:49:02.819 --> 00:49:06.979
What regulatory and trust constraints shape Indian e-commerce design?

613
00:49:07.259 --> 00:49:08.624
Thirty-second answer:

614
00:49:08.904 --> 00:49:26.163
Consumer-protection e-commerce rules on disclosures and certain flash-sale practices, grievance redressal, GST invoicing per seller, RBI rules for card data and payment aggregators, and personal-data protection, all to be checked against current text (VERIFY).

615
00:49:27.513 --> 00:49:28.622
Question 20.

616
00:49:28.902 --> 00:49:34.043
Compare the design priorities of an Indian sale-day platform with a global marketplace.

617
00:49:34.323 --> 00:49:35.689
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:49:35.969 --> 00:49:55.126
Both need cells, reservation and resilience; the Indian sale adds extreme synchronised spikes, COD or RTO economics, slow-network design, household-level limits, bank-offer complexity and vernacular reach, so edge, client and risk design matter as much as back-end scale.

619
00:49:56.476 --> 00:49:57.372
Part 9.

620
00:49:57.652 --> 00:49:59.060
Summary and Practice.

621
00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:01.398
Key takeaways.

622
00:50:01.678 --> 00:50:05.091
Sync clients to server time and re-sync before unlock.

623
00:50:05.371 --> 00:50:07.462
Never poll the origin (L1).

624
00:50:07.742 --> 00:50:13.054
Price COD risk with bands and confirmation, and count new-customer value (L2).

625
00:50:13.334 --> 00:50:15.915
Budget payloads and connections for 2G.

626
00:50:16.195 --> 00:50:19.246
Setup round trips dominate small payloads (L3).

627
00:50:19.526 --> 00:50:24.774
Enforce limits on structured household keys, not accounts or fuzzy text (L4).

628
00:50:26.124 --> 00:50:31.500
Sale days are won by readiness: capacity, dependencies, switches, freeze, war room.

629
00:50:32.850 --> 00:50:33.831
Cheat sheet.

630
00:50:34.111 --> 00:50:35.455
Here is a quick table:

631
00:50:35.735 --> 00:50:37.122
Problem, and Pattern.

